                  %a{:href => "#compatibility_of_quantization_with_symplectic_reduction"} Compatibility of quantization with symplectic reduction
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#characteristic_central_extensions"} Characteristic central extensions
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ExamplesSchroedingerRepresentation"} Schrödinger representation
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#khler_manifolds"} Kähler manifolds
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ExampleThe2Sphere"} The 2-sphere
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ExamplesTori"} Tori
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#theta_functions"} Theta functions
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#quantization_of_chernsimons_theory"} Quantization of Chern-Simons theory
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#quantization_of_loop_groups__of_the_wzw_model"} Quantization of loop groups / of the WZW model
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#quantization_in_gromovwitten_theory"} Quantization in Gromov-Witten theory
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#quantization_of_the_bosonic_string_model"}
                    Quantization of the bosonic string
                    = succeed "-model" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi σ
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#References"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesGeneral"} General
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#holographic_quantization"} Holographic quantization
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#quantization"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi Spin
                            %mi ℂ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spin^{\mathbb{C}}
                    \-Quantization
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#examples_2"} Examples
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesRelationToDeformation"} Relation to deformation quantization
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#relation_to_path_integral_quantization"} Relation to path integral quantization
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesBRST"} Geometric BRST quantization
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesSupergeometric"} Supergeometric version
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#of_presymplectic_manifolds"} Of presymplectic manifolds
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#of_generalized_complex_manifolds"} Of generalized complex manifolds
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_higher_differential_geometry"} In higher differential geometry
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Geometric quantization is one formalization of the notion of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanical+system"} classical mechanical system
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            to a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanical+system"} quantum mechanical system
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            it focuses on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+states"} spaces of states
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+picture"} Schrödinger picture
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
          %h3#ingredients Ingredients
          %p
            With a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            regarded as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanical+system"} classical mechanical system
            %em geometric quantization
            produces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of this to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanical+system"} quantum mechanical system
            by
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                realize the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
                as the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
                of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+U%281%29-bundle"} principal U(1)-bundle
                with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
                (which requires the form to have integral
                = succeed "):" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/periods"} periods
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+circle+bundle"} prequantum circle bundle
            %li
              %p
                choose a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarization"} polarization
                – a splitting of the abstract
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
                into “coordinates” and “momenta”;
          %p and then form
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/states"} states
                as the space of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+bundle"} associated
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
                which depend only on the “coordinates” (not on the “momenta”);
            %li
              %p
                associate with every function on the symplectic manifold – every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
                – a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+operator"} linear operator
                on this Hilbert space.
          %h3#HistoryAndVariants History and variants
          %p
            The approach is due to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexandre+Kirillov"} Alexandre Kirillov
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbit+method"}> orbit method
            ”),
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bertram+Kostant"} Bertram Kostant
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Marie+Souriau"} Jean-Marie Souriau
            %a{:href => "#ReferencesGeneral"} References
            below. It is closely related to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berezin+quantization"} Berezin quantization
            and the subject of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+states"} coherent states
          %p
            %img{:alt => "Prequantum flow chart", :src => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/SouriauPrequantumFlowChart.jpg"}/
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Souriau74"}> Souriau 74, fig 1
            )
          %p
            In a long term project
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alan+Weinstein"} Alan Weinstein
            and many of his students have followed the idea that the true story behind geometric quantization crucially involves
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+Lie+groupoids"} symplectic Lie groupoids
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+symplectic+geometry"} higher symplectic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization+of+symplectic+groupoids"} geometric quantization of symplectic groupoids
            for more on this.
          %p
            More generally, there is
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometric+quantization"} higher geometric quantization
          %h3#overview Overview
          %blockquote
            %p
              This overview is taken from (
              %a{:href => "#Baez"}> Baez
              ).
          %p
            %em Geometric quantization
            is a tool for understanding the relation between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+physics"} classical physics
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum physics
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                We start with a
                = succeed ":" do
                  %em
                    classical
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                with a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic structure
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ω
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            %li
              %p
                Then we do
                = succeed ":" do
                  %em prequantization
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                over
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                whose curvature equals
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mi ω
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \omega
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                is called the
                = succeed "." do
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
              %p
                %strong Warning:
                we can only do this step if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
                satisfies the
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr-Sommerfeld+leaf"} Bohr-Sommerfeld condition
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 2
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega/2\pi
                defines an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+cohomology"} integral cohomology
                class. If this condition holds,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                are determined up to
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi H
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_0
                of square-integrable
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                is called the
                = succeed "." do
                  %em prequantum Hilbert space
                = succeed ":" do
                  %em prequantize classical observables
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                to an operator on
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi H
                          %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_0
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutator"} commutator
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %li
              %p
                To cut down the prequantum Hilbert space, we need to choose a
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarization"} polarization
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi P
                        %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_x
                of the complexified
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent space
                at
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                = succeed "," do
                  %em quantum Hilbert space
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi H
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                that give zero when we take their
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+derivative"} covariant derivative
                at any point
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                in the direction of any vector in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi P
                          %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_x
              %p
                %strong Warning:
                for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                to be a polarization, there are some crucial technical conditions we impose on the subspaces
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi P
                          %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_x
                = succeed ":" do
                  %em isotropic
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
                must vanish on them. Second, they must be
                = succeed ":" do
                  %em Lagrangian
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                = succeed "." do
                  %em integrable
            %li
              %p
                The easiest sort of polarization to understand is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %em real polarization
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi P
                        %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_x
                come from subspaces of the tangent space by complexification. It boils down to this: a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+polarization"} real polarization
                is an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integrable+distribution"} integrable distribution
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                on the classical phase space where each space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi P
                        %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_x
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+subspace"} Lagrangian subspace
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+space"} tangent space
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi T
                          %mi x
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_x X
            %li
              %p
                To understand this rigamarole, one must study examples! First, it’s good to understand how good old
                %em Schrödinger quantization
                fits into this framework. Remember, in Schrödinger quantization we take our classical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                to be the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cotangent+bundle"} cotangent bundle
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi T
                        %mo *
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^* M
                of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                called the
                = succeed "." do
                  %em classical configuration space
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/square-integrable+functions"} square-integrable functions
                on
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              %p
                Modulo some technical trickery, we get this example when we run the above machinery and use a certain god-given real polarization on
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo =
                        %msup
                          %mi T
                          %mo *
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = T^*M
            %li
              %p
                It’s also good to study the
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Bargmann–Segal representation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi ℂ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = \mathbb{C}^n
                with its god-given symplectic structure (the imaginary part of the inner product) and using the god-given
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Kähler polarization
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℂ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^n
                which are square-integrable with respect to a Gaussian measure centered at the origin.
            %li
              %p
                The next step is to
                = succeed "," do
                  %em quantize classical observables
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi H
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutator"} commutator
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
                from the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
                of symplectic manifolds to the category of Hilbert spaces – but for that one needs to learn a bit about
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
          %h3#basic_jargon Basic Jargon
          %p
            Here are some definitions of important terms. Unfortunately they are defined using other terms that you might not understand. If you are really mystified, you need to read some books on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            and the math of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            before proceeding.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong> complexification
                \: We can tensor a real vector space with the complex numbers and get a complex vector space; this process is called complexification. For example, we can complexify the tangent space at some point of a manifold, which amounts to forming the space of complex linear combinations of tangent vectors at that point.
            %li
              %p
                %strong> distribution
                \: The word “distribution” means many different things in mathematics, but here’s one: a “distribution”
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                on a manifold
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is a choice of a subspace
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_x
                of each tangent space
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi T
                          %mi p
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_p X
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %li
              %p
                %strong> Hamiltonian vector field
                \: Given a manifold
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                with a symplectic structure
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ω
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: X \to \mathbb{R}
                can be thought of as a “Hamiltonian”, meaning physically that we think of it as the energy function and let it give rise to a flow on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                describing the time evolution of states. Mathematically speaking, this flow is generated by a vector field
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v(f)
                called the “Hamiltonian vector field” associated to
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi d
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\omega(-, v(f)) = d f
              %p
                In other words, for any vector field
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                we have
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi u
                      %mo ,
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi d
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi u
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\omega(u,v(f)) = d f(u) = u f
              %p
                The vector field
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v(f)
                is guaranteed to exist by the fact that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
                is nondegenerate.
            %li
              %p
                %strong>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integrable+distribution"} integrable distribution
                \: A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distribution+of+subspaces"} distribution of subspaces
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
                on a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is “integrable” if at least locally, there is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foliation"} foliation
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                by submanifolds such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_x
                is the tangent space of the submanifold containing the point
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %li
              %p
                %strong>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+cohomology"} integral cohomology
                  class
                \: Any closed
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+p-form"} differential p-form
                on a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                defines an element of the
                = succeed "th" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham cohomology
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                = succeed "th" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+group"} cohomology group
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
                on any Hermitian line bundle over
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                = succeed "-form" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi π
                        %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 \pi i
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+class"} Chern class
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle"} bundle
                up to
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            %li
              %p
                %strong>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"}> Poisson bracket
                  s
                \: Given a symplectic structure on a manifold
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                and given two smooth functions on that manifold, say
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{f,g\}
                on the manifold, called the Poisson bracket of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
              %p
                This trick works as follows: given any smooth function
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                we can take its differential
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d f
                = succeed "-form." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi v
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v(f)
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi d
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\omega(-,v(f)) = d f
              %p Using this we define
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo =
                      %mi ω
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\{f,g\} = \omega(v(f),v(g))
              %p
                It’s easy to check that we also have
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi f
                        %mo ,
                        %mi g
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mo =
                        %mi d
                        %mi g
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi v
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi v
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi g
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{f,g\} = d g(v(f)) = v(f) g
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{f,g\}
                says how much
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
                changes as we differentiate it in the direction of the Hamiltonian vector field generated by
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              %p
                In the familiar case where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                is
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{2n}
                with momentum and position coordinates
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_i
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi q
                          %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q_i
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
                work out to be
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo =
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mi i
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                          %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                          %msub
                            %mi p
                            %mi i
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                          %mi g
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                          %msub
                            %mi q
                            %mi i
                      %mo −
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                          %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                          %msub
                            %mi q
                            %mi i
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                          %mi g
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                          %msub
                            %mi p
                            %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\{f,g\} = \sum_i \frac{d f}{d p_i} \frac{d g}{d q_i} - \frac{d f}{d q_i}\frac{d g}{d p_i}
            %li
              %p
                %strong> square-integrable sections
                \: We can define an inner product on the sections of a Hermitian line bundle over a manifold
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                with a symplectic structure. The symplectic structure defines a volume form which lets us do the necessary integral. A section whose inner product with itself is finite is said to be square-integrable. Such sections form a Hilbert space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi H
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_0
                called the “prequantum Hilbert space”. It is a kind of preliminary version of the Hilbert space we get when we quantize the classical system whose phase space is
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                %strong> symplectic structure
                \: A symplectic structure on a manifold
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                is a closed
                = succeed "-form" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
                which is nondegenerate in the sense that for any nonzero tangent vector
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
                at any point of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
                at that point for which
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi w
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi u
                      %mo ,
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w(u,v)
                is nonzero.
            %li
              %p
                %strong>
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
                \: The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
                is the group of unit complex numbers. Given a complex line bundle
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                with an inner product on each fiber
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi L
                          %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_x
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
                connection on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                is a connection such that parallel translation preserves the inner product.
            %li
              %p
                %strong> vertical vectors
                \: Given a bundle
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                over a manifold
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                is vertical if it projects to zero down on
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p Geometric quantization involves two steps
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#GeometricPrequantization"} Geometric prequantization
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#GeometricQuantizationProper"}> Geometric quantization proper
                \.
          %h3#GeometricPrequantization Geometric prequantization
          %h4#prequantum_line_bundle Prequantum line bundle
          %p
            Given the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+circle+bundle"} prequantum circle bundle
            for it is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundle+with+connection"} circle bundle with connection
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
          %p
            In other words, prequantization is a lift of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            through the curvature-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+sequence"} exact sequence
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
            (see there).
          %p
            The multiple of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+class"} Chern class
            of this line bundle is identified with the inverse
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck+constant"} Planck constant
          %h4#prequantum_states Prequantum states
          %p
            A
            %em prequantum state
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+bundle"} prequantum bundle
          %p
            This becomes a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum state
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavefunction"} wavefunction
            if
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarization"} polarized
            (…).
          %h4#prequantum_operators Prequantum operators
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi conn
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla : X \to \mathbf{B} U(1)_{conn}
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} with connection
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Γ
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_X(E)
            for its space of smooth
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+space+of+states"} prequantum space of states
          #PrequantumOperator.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+operators"}> prequantum operators
                )
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in C^\infty(X, \mathbb{C})
              a function on phase space, the corresponding
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+operator+%28in+geometric+quantization%29"} quantum operator
              is the linear map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\hat f \colon \Gamma_X(E) \to \Gamma_X(E)
            %p given by
            #eq:FormulaForPrequantumOperator.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo ↦
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mo ∇
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mi f
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo +
                    %mi f
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi ψ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\psi \mapsto -i \nabla_{v_f} \psi + f \cdot \psi
                    \\,,
            %p where
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v_f
                  is the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+vector+field"} Hamiltonian vector field
                  corresponding to
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi f
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∇
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi v
                              %mi f
                        %mo :
                        %msub
                          %mi Γ
                          %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi Γ
                          %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla_{v_f} : \Gamma_X(E) \to \Gamma_X(E)
                  is the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+derivative"} covariant derivative
                  of sections along
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v_f
                  for the given choice of prequantum connection;
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo :
                        %msub
                          %mi Γ
                          %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi Γ
                          %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \cdot (-) : \Gamma_X(E) \to \Gamma_X(E)
                  is the operation of degreewise multiplication pf sections.
          #OriginOfTheFormulaForPrequantumOperators.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              %strong
                (origin of the formulas for
                = succeed ")*" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+operators"} prequantum operators
            %p
              The formula
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:FormulaForPrequantumOperator"} (1)
              may look a bit mysterious on first sight. The correction term to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+derivative"} covariant derivative
              appearing in this formula is ultimately due to the fact that with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+vector+field"} Hamiltonian vector field
              corresponding to a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_v
              via
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mi v
                    %mi ω
                    %mo =
                    %mi d
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\iota_v \omega = d H_v
            %p
              then the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+derivative"} Lie derivative
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta
              (the symplectic potentiation, related by
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mi θ
                      %mo =
                      %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \theta = \omega
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℒ
                      %mi v
                    %mi θ
                    %mo =
                    %mi d
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi H
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{L}_v \theta = d \tilde H_v
            %p for
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi H
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mi v
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mi v
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mi v
                    %mi θ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\tilde H_v = H_v + \iota_v \theta
                    \\,.
            %p
              Here the second term on the right is what yields the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+derivative"} covariant derivative
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:FormulaForPrequantumOperator"} (1)
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:FormulaForPrequantumOperator"} (1)
            %p
              A derivation of these formulas from first principles is given in (
              %a{:href => "#FiorenzaRogersSchreiber13a"}> Fiorenza-Rogers-Schreiber 13a
              , example 3.2.3 and remark 3.3.16).
          %h3#GeometricQuantizationProper Geometric quantization
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+bundle"} prequantum bundle
            as above, the actual step of genuine
            %em geometric quantization
            consists first of forming
            %em half
            its space of sections in a certain sense. Physically this means passing to the space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavefunctions"} wavefunctions
            that depend only on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+positions"} canonical positions
            but not on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+momenta"} canonical momenta
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroups"} subgroups
            of the group of (exponentiated)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+operators"} prequantum operators
            are made to descend to this space of quantum states, these are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+operators"} quantum operators
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
          %h4#QuantumStates Quantum states
          %p
            Historically, the traditional way to formalize the formation of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of quantum states
            is as a 3-step process
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                choose a
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a{:href => "#Polarizations"} Polarization
            %li
              %p
                choose a
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a{:href => "#MetaplecticCorrection"} Metaplectic correction
            %li
              %p
                form the induced
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of quantum states
                as the space of polarized sections of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
                tensored a certain half-form bundle.
          %p This we discuss at
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#Polarizations"} Quantum state space as space of polarized sections
          %p
            This traditional route via polarizations and metaplectic corrections has the disadvantage that mathematically it is not a very natural operation. However, under mild conditions it turns out to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalent
            to the following mathematically very natural construction
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                choose a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KU-orientation"} KU-orientation
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+structure"} spin^c structure
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                compatible with the given prequantum bundle;
            %li
              %p
                take the space of quantum states to be the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/push-forward+in+generalized+cohomology"} push-forward
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+K-theory"} complex K-theory
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
                to the point, hence the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index"} index
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+Dirac+operator"} spin^c Dirac operator
                twisted by the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
          %p
            This general
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization+by+push-forward"} geometric quantization by push-forward
            is discussed below at
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#AsIndexOfSpinCDiracOperator"} Quantum space of states as index of a Dirac operator
              \.
          %p
            In the special case that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
            admits a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+polarization"} Kähler polarization
            this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization+by+push-forward"} push-forward quantization
            has a direct expression in terms of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+geometry"} complex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
            and of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dolbeault+operator"} Dolbeault operator
            of the prequantum
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+line+bundle"} holomorphic line bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metaplectic+correction"} metaplectic correction
            is precisely a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} spin structure
            (a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Theta+characteristic"}> Theta characteristic
            ”) and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index"} index
            is now that of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dolbeault-Dirac+operator"} Dolbeault-Dirac operator
            which is equivalently just the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler+characteristic"} Euler characteristic
            of the holomorphic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#EulerCharacteristicOfSheafCohomology"} Quantum state space as Euler characteristic of prequantum sheaf cohomology
          %p and
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#IndexOfDolbeaultDiracOperator"} Quantum state space as index of the Dolbeault-Dirac operator
              \.
          %h5#Polarizations Quantum state space as space of polarized sections
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \omega)
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+polarization"} Kähler polarization
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mo ⊂
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P} \subset T_{\mathcal{C}} X
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mo ∩
                    %mover
                      %mi 𝒫
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P} \cap \overline{\mathcal{P}} = 0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metaplectic+correction"} metaplectic correction
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒫
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/half-density"} half-density
            bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msqrt
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi 𝒫
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sqrt{\Omega \mathcal{P}}
            along
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒫
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}
          %p
            Let now
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (L,\nabla)
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
          #PolarizedQuantumStates.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of quantum states
              of the prequantum bundle defined by the choice of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+polarization"} Kähler polarization
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒫
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metaplectic+correction"} metaplectic correction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %mi Ω
                        %mi 𝒫
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sqrt{\Omega \mathcal{P}}
              is the space of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %mi Ω
                        %mi 𝒫
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \otimes \sqrt{\Omega \mathcal{P}}
              which are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+derivative"} covariant derivative
              covariantly constant with respect to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
              along
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi 𝒫
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\mathcal{P}}
              and square integrable with respect to the induced
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
              over
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mover
                        %mi 𝒫
                        %mo ¯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X/\overline{\mathcal{P}}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℋ
                      %mi pol
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mrow
                        %mo {
                        %mi ψ
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Γ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi L
                        %mo ⊗
                        %msqrt
                          %mrow
                            %mi Ω
                            %mi 𝒫
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %msub
                          %mo ∇
                          %mover
                            %mi 𝒫
                            %mo ¯
                        %mi ψ
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                        %mo }
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{H}_{pol}
                    \\colon
                    L^2
                    \\left(
                    \\left\{
                    \\psi \in \Gamma(L \otimes \sqrt{\Omega \mathcal{P}})
                    \\;|\;
                    \\nabla_{\overline{\mathcal{P}}} \psi = 0
                    \\right\}
                    \\right)
                    \\,.
          %p
            For instance (
            %a{:href => "#BatesWeinstein"}> Bates-Weinstein, def. 7.17
            ).
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              In the case of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+polarization"} Kähler polarization
              it is useful to write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^{n,0}
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+line+bundle"} canonical line bundle
              of the polarization and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sqrt{\Omega^{n,0}}
              for the corresponding choice of
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metaplectic+correction"} metaplectic correction
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Theta+characteristic"} Theta characteristic
          %h5#EulerCharacteristicOfSheafCohomology Quantum state space as Euler characteristic of prequantum sheaf cohomology
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            be a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact topological space
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\omega
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒫
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+polarization"} Kähler polarization
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msqrt
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sqrt{\Omega^{n,0}}
            a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metaplectic+correction"} metaplectic correction
          #EulerQuantumStates.num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              The corresponding Euler quantum Hilbert space is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+vector+space"} virtual vector space
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℋ
                      %mi Euler
                    %mo ≔
                    %munderover
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi k
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mi ω
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{H}_{Euler}
                    \\coloneqq
                    \\bigoplus_{k = 0}^{n} (-1)^k \, H^{0,k}(X,L_\omega \otimes \sqrt{\Omega^{n,0}})
                    \\,,
            %p
              which is the alternating
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dolbeault+cohomology"} Dolbeault cohomology
              space of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mi ω
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\omega \otimes \sqrt{\Omega^{n,0}}
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
              with the
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metaplectic+correction"} metaplectic correction
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Theta+characteristic"} Theta characteristic
          #SectionsAndEulerQuantizationCoincidesOnPositiveBundles.num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kodaira+vanishing+theorem"} Kodaira vanishing theorem
              asserts that if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mi ω
                    %msup
                      %msqrt
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi Ω
                            %mrow
                              %mi n
                              %mo ,
                              %mn 0
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\omega \sqrt{\Omega^{n,0}}^{-1}
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+line+bundle"} positive line bundle
              then all the higher
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+groups"} cohomology groups
              in the above expression vanish. Therefore in this case the definition coincides with that via polarizations in def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PolarizedQuantumStates"}
              above.
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mi ω
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msup
                        %msqrt
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi Ω
                              %mrow
                                %mi n
                                %mo ,
                                %mn 0
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi positive
                      %mo )
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msub
                        %mi ℋ
                        %mi pol
                      %mo ≃
                      %msub
                        %mi ℋ
                        %mi Euler
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\left(
                    L_\omega \otimes \sqrt{\Omega^{n,0}}^{-1}
                    \\;
                    positive
                    \\right)
                    \\Rightarrow
                    \\left(
                    \\mathcal{H}_{pol} \simeq \mathcal{H}_{Euler}
                    \\right)
                    \\,.
          %h5#IndexOfDolbeaultDiracOperator Quantum state space as index of Dolbeault-Dirac operator
          %p
            Suppose again that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega, L_\omega)
            is equipped with a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+polarization"} Kähler polarization
          %p
            We need the following general fact on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structures"} spin structures
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+manifolds"} Kähler manifolds
          #SpinStructuresOnKaehlerManifolds.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              A choice of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} spin structure
              on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+manifold"} Kähler manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              (of real dimension
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2n
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/square+root"} square root
              (“
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Theta+characteristic"}> Theta characteristic
              ”)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sqrt{\Omega^{n,0}}
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+line+bundle"} canonical line bundle
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^{n,0}
            %p
              Given such a choice, there is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphism"} natural isomorphism
              between the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor+bundle"} spinor bundle
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_X
              and the (anti-)holomorphic form bundle tensored by this square root
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mo •
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    S \simeq \Omega^{0,\bullet} \otimes \sqrt{\Omega^{n,0}}
                    \\,.
            %p
              Finally, the corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dolbeault-Dirac+operator"} Dolbeault-Dirac operator
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mo ∂
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo +
                      %msup
                        %mover
                          %mo ∂
                          %mo ¯
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\partial} + \overline{\partial}^\ast
          %p
            See at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "spin+structure#OverAKahlerManifold"} spin structure – Over a Kähler manifold
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_4 Remark
            %p
              It follows that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              which is twisted by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
              on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+line+bundle"} holomorphic line bundle
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\omega
              is of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mo ∂
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo ∇
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %msubsup
                      %mover
                        %mo ∂
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo ∇
                      %mo *
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mi even
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi odd
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mi ω
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msqrt
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi Ω
                            %mrow
                              %mi n
                              %mo ,
                              %mn 0
                      %mo )
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mi odd
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi even
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mi ω
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msqrt
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi Ω
                            %mrow
                              %mi n
                              %mo ,
                              %mn 0
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\overline{\partial}_\nabla + \overline{\partial}^\ast_\nabla
                    \\;\colon\;
                    \\Omega^{0,even/odd}\left(X, \;L_\omega \otimes \sqrt{\Omega^{n,0}}\right)
                    \\to
                    \\Omega^{0,odd/even}\left(X, \; L_\omega \otimes \sqrt{\Omega^{n,0}}\right)
                    \\,.
            %p
              Observe how from the point of view of just the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dolbeault+operator"} Dolbeault operator
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metaplectic+correction"} metaplectically corrected
              prequantum line bundle
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mi ω
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msqrt
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi Ω
                            %mrow
                              %mi n
                              %mo ,
                              %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\omega \otimes \sqrt{\Omega^{n,0}}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
              it is just twisting by
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\omega
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sqrt{\Omega^{n,0}}
              induces the isomorphism between the antiholomorphic differential form bundle and the actual
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor+bundle"} spinor bundle
              over the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+manifold"} Kähler manifold
          #DolbeaultDiracSpacesOfStates.num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              The
              %em Dolbeault-Dirac
              space of quantum states of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega, L_\omega)
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index"} index
              of the
              = succeed "-twisted" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\omega
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dolbeault-Dirac+operator"} Dolbeault-Dirac operator
              (the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Todd+genus"} Todd genus
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℋ
                      %mi DolbDir
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi index
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mo ∂
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo ∇
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %msubsup
                      %mover
                        %mo ∂
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo ∇
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{H}_{DolbDir}
                    \\coloneqq
                    index( \overline{\partial}_\nabla + \overline{\partial}_\nabla^\ast )
                    \\,.
          #DolbeaultDiracAgreesWithEuler.num_remark
            %h6#remark_5 Remark
            %p
              This definition agrees with that by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
              in def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EulerQuantumStates"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℋ
                      %mi Euler
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi ℋ
                      %mi DolbDirac
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{H}_{Euler} \simeq \mathcal{H}_{DolbDirac}
                    \\,.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_6 Remark
            %p
              What before in the quantization prescription
              %a{:href => "#Polarizations"} by polarization
              and
              %a{:href => "#EulerCharacteristicOfSheafCohomology"} by abelian sheaf cohomology
              was the “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metaplectic+correction"}> metaplectic correction
              ” introduced “by hand” is now naturally part of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
              of prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SpinStructuresOnKaehlerManifolds"}
              which identifies the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
              with the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dolbeault-Dirac+operator"} Dolbeault-Dirac operator
          %h5#AsIndexOfSpinCDiracOperator
            Quantum state spaces as index of the
            = succeed "-Dirac" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Spin
                      %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spin^c
          %p Finally we come to the true definition of geometric quantization, the most general and at the same time most natural one which contains the above as special cases.
          %p
            The actual definition is def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#KTheoreticQuantization"}
            below. Here we lead up to it by spelling out the ingredients.
          %p
            We need the following general facts about
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+Dirac+operators"} spin^c Dirac operators
          #SpinCAndDeterminantLineBundle.num_defn
            %h6#definition_6 Definition
            %p
              For
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec"} spin^c
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Spin
                      %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spin^c(n)
              is equivalently
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
                  of the ordinary
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+group"} spin group
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Spin
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spin(n)
                  with the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
                  over the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+of+order+2"} group of order 2
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Spin
                          %mi c
                        %mo ≃
                        %mi Spin
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %munder
                          %mo ×
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi ℤ
                              %mn 2
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        Spin^c \simeq Spin(n) \underset{\mathbb{Z}_2}{\times} U(1)
              %li
                %p
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space+object"} loop space object
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Spin
                          %mi c
                        %mo ≃
                        %mi Ω
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %msup
                          %mi Spin
                          %mi c
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        Spin^c \simeq \Omega \mathbf{B} Spin^c
                %p
                  of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber+product"} homotopy fiber product
                  of the universal smooth
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+Stiefel-Whitney+class"} second Stiefel-Whitney class
                  and the mod-2 reduction of the universal
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+Chern+class"} first Chern class
                  in
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoids"} smooth infinity-groupoids
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mtable
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                  %mi B
                                %msup
                                  %mi Spin
                                  %mi c
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi n
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mtd
                                %mover
                                  %mo →
                                  %mi det
                              %mtd
                                %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                  %mi B
                                %mi U
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mn 1
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mo ⇙
                                  %mo ≃
                              %mtd
                                %msup
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                  %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                    %mrow
                                      %msub
                                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                          %mi c
                                        %mn 1
                                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                                      %mi mod
                                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                                      %mn 2
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                  %mi B
                                %mi SO
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi n
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mtd
                                %mover
                                  %mo →
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                        %mi w
                                      %mn 2
                              %mtd
                                %msup
                                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                    %mi B
                                  %mn 2
                                %msub
                                  %mi ℤ
                                  %mn 2
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo .
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\array{
                        \\mathbf{B}Spin^c(n) &amp;\stackrel{det}{\to}&amp; \mathbf{B}U(1)
                        \\\
                        \\downarrow &amp;\swArrow_\simeq&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{\mathbf{c}_1 \, mod \, 2}}
                        \\\
                        \\mathbf{B}SO(n) &amp;\stackrel{\mathbf{w}_2}{\to}&amp; \mathbf{B}^2 \mathbb{Z}_2
                        }
                        \\,.
            %p
              Here the top horizontal map is called the universal
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/determinant+line+bundle"} determinant line bundle
              map.
          %p
            See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+group"} spin^c group
            for more details.
          #SpinCInComponents.num_remark
            %h6#remark_7 Remark
            %p
              It follows that if we represent elements of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Spin
                      %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi SO
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %munder
                      %mo ×
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ℤ
                          %mn 2
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spin^c(n) \simeq SO(n) \underset{\mathbb{Z}_2}{\times} U(1)
              as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+classes"} equivalence classes
              of pairs
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (g,c)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/determinant+line+bundle"} determinant line bundle
              map of def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SpinCAndDeterminantLineBundle"}
              is given by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi det
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↦
                    %mn 2
                    %mi c
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    det \;\colon \; (g,c) \mapsto 2c
                    \\,,
            %p
              where on the right we write the group operation in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
              additively.
            %p This factor of 2 on the right is crucial in all of the following.
          #SpinCStructure.num_defn
            %h6#definition_7 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} oriented
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+structure"} spin^c structure
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a lift
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi τ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat \tau_X
              of the classifying map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi τ
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi SO
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau_X \colon X \to \mathbf{B} SO(n)
              of its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
              through the left vertical map in def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SpinCAndDeterminantLineBundle"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %msup
                              %mi Spin
                              %mi c
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mover
                                      %mi τ
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                                    %mi X
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi τ
                                  %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi SO
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp;&amp; \mathbf{B} Spin^c(n)
                    \\\
                    \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{\hat \tau_X}}\nearrow &amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    X &amp;\stackrel{\tau_X}{\to}&amp; \mathbf{B}SO(n)
                    }
                    \\,.
          #SpinCWithDivisibleDetIsSpinTensorLine.num_remark
            %h6#remark_8 Remark
            %p
              In words this says that a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+structure"} spin^c structure
              on an oriented manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a choice of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+U%281%29-bundle"} principal U(1)-bundle
              or equivalenty of hermitian
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+line+bundle"} complex line bundle
              such that its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+Chern+class"} first Chern class
              modulo 2 equals the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+Stiefel-Whitney+class"} second Stiefel-Whitney class
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi w
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w_2
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi H
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^2(X,\mathbb{Z}_2)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              has a genuine
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} spin structure
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/determinant+line+bundle"} determinant line bundle
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+structure"} spin^c structure
              (in the sens of def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SpinCAndDeterminantLineBundle"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+Chern+class"} first Chern class
              that is divisible by 2, then there is an actual
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+structure"} spin^c structure
            %p
              We can formalize this statement as follows: there is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+square"} commuting square
              of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi Spin
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ×
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mn 2
                                %mo ⋅
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo ∘
                                %msub
                                  %mi p
                                  %mn 2
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                      %mi c
                                    %mn 1
                                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                                  %mi mod
                                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                                  %mn 2
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi SO
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                    %mi w
                                  %mn 2
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                %mi B
                              %mn 2
                            %msub
                              %mi ℤ
                              %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\mathbf{B}( Spin(n) \times U(1) ) &amp;\stackrel{((2 \cdot)\circ p_2}{\to}&amp;
                    \\mathbf{B}U(1)
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{\mathbf{c}_1 \, mod\, 2}}
                    \\\
                    \\mathbf{B}SO(n) &amp;\stackrel{\mathbf{w}^2}{\to}&amp; \mathbf{B}^2 \mathbb{Z}_2
                    }
            %p
              similar to the one in def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SpinCAndDeterminantLineBundle"}
              but crucially different in that here we have just the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spin(n)
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
              in the top left. In the standard presentation of these objects this diagram commutes on the nose (filled by a canonical
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \in \mathbb{Z}_2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi Spin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}Spin(n)
              is essentially by definition such that the second SW class trivializes on it, and the top-right one because first multiplying by 2 and then reducing mod 2 is 0.
            %p
              Since the diagram commutes, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
              of the above
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullback"} homotopy pullback
              says that there is a canonically induced map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi Spin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Spin
                      %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (p_1, p_2) \;\colon\; Spin(n) \times U(1) \to Spin^c(n)
                    \\,.
            %p
              Of course this is just the evident
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection"} projection
              which on elements is simply the identity
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↦
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (g,c) \mapsto (g,c)
                    \\,,
            %p
              only that on the right we regard the pair
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (g,c)
              as a placeholder for its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+class"} equivalence class
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
              over
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
            %p
              Moreover, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullback"} homotopy pullback
              says that every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+structure"} spin^c structure
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi τ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat \tau_c
              whose underlying
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/determinant+line+bundle"} determinant line bundle
              is such that its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+Chern+class"} first Chern class
              is divisible by 2 actually factors through this map, hence that
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  it is just the product of an ordinary
                  = succeed "-principal" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Spin
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spin
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi Spin
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ×
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi p
                                  %mn 2
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %msub
                                    %mi p
                                    %mn 1
                                  %mo ,
                                  %msub
                                    %mi p
                                    %mn 2
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mrow
                                %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                  %mi B
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mn 2
                                %mo ⋅
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %msup
                              %mi Spin
                              %mi c
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi det
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi pb
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi c
                                    %mn 1
                                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                                  %mi mod
                                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                                  %mn 2
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi SO
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                    %mi w
                                  %mn 2
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                %mi B
                              %mn 2
                            %msub
                              %mi ℤ
                              %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\mathbf{B}(Spin(n) \times U(1)) &amp;\stackrel{p_2}{\to}&amp; \mathbf{B}U(1)
                    \\\
                    {}^{\mathllap{(p_1,p_2)}}\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\mathbf{B}(2 \cdot)}
                    \\\
                    \\mathbf{B}Spin^c(n) &amp;\stackrel{det}{\to}&amp; \mathbf{B}U(1)
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;(pb)&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{c_1\,mod\,2}}
                    \\\
                    \\mathbf{B}SO(n) &amp;\stackrel{\mathbf{w}_2}{\to}&amp; \mathbf{B}^2 \mathbb{Z}_2
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p This is the crucial relation by which the K-theoretic quantization will harmonize with the above Euler- and Dolbeault- quantization, discussed below.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_8 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              an even number, write
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi n
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mn 2
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Delta_n \simeq \mathbb{C}^{2^{n/2}}
            %p
              for the canonical complex
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+representation"} spin representation
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreducible+representations"} irreducible representations
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi n
                    %mo ≃
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                    %mo ⊕
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Delta_n \simeq \Delta_n^+ \oplus \Delta_n^-
                    \\,.
            %p
              Then the
              %strong
                canonical
                = succeed "-" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec"} spin^c
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation"} representation
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi Spin
                      %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\rho \colon Spin^c(n) \times \Delta_n \to \Delta_n
            %p
              is the one given in the components of remark
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SpinCInComponents"}
              by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↦
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    ((g,c), \psi) \mapsto (g(\psi)) \cdot c
            %p
              (first act with the spin-component in the usual way and then multiply by
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi ℂ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in U(1) \hookrightarrow \mathbb{C}
          %p
            As discussed at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation"} representation
            we may think of this as a morphism of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+groupoids"} smooth groupoids
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stacks"}> stacks
            ) of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msup
                    %mi Spin
                    %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Mod
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Vect
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\rho \colon \mathbf{B} Spin^c(n) \to \mathbb{C}\mathbf{Mod} \simeq \mathbb{C}\mathbf{Vect}
                  \\,.
          #SpinCSpinorBundle.num_defn
            %h6#definition_9 Definition
            %p
              Given a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+structure"} spin^c structure
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mover
                          %mi τ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %msup
                        %mi Spin
                        %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat \tau_X \colon X \to \mathbf{B}Spin^c(n)
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SpinCStructure"}
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+bundle"} associated
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor+bundle"} spinor bundle
              is the one modulated by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mi c
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mover
                            %mi τ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                          %mi X
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %msup
                      %mi Spin
                      %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi ρ
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Vect
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    S^c
                    \\colon
                    X \stackrel{\hat \tau_X}{\to} \mathbf{B}Spin^c(n)
                    \\stackrel{\rho}{\to} \mathbb{C}\mathbf{Vect}
                    \\,.
          %p Crucial for the comparison of the K-theoretic quantization to be defined in a moment and the above Euler/Dolbeault quantization is the following:
          #SpinCSpinorsAsTensorProductWhenDetDivisible.num_remark
            %h6#remark_9 Remark
            %p
              By remark
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SpinCWithDivisibleDetIsSpinTensorLine"}
              it follows that in the case that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/determinant+line+bundle"} determinant line bundle
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+structure"} spin^c structure
              is divisible by 2, hence in the case that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi det
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mover
                          %mi τ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi mod
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi H
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_1(det(\hat \tau_X)) = 0 \,mod\, 2 \in H^2(X,\mathbb{Z}_2)
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Spin
                        %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spin^c
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor+bundle"} spinor bundle
              of def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SpinCSpinorBundle"}
              is just the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
              of the ordinary underlying
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor+bundle"} spinor bundle
              with
              %em half
              the
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/determinant+line+bundle"} determinant line bundle
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi det
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mover
                          %mi τ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi mod
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mn 2
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mi c
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi S
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msqrt
                        %mrow
                          %mi det
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mover
                              %mi τ
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                            %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\left( c_1(det(\hat \tau_X)) = 0 \,mod\, 2 \right)
                    \\;\Rightarrow\;
                    \\left(S^c \simeq S \otimes \sqrt{det(\hat \tau_X)}\right)
                    \\,.
          %p
            Let now
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            be a (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+manifold"}> pre-
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi ω
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (L_{\omega}, \nabla)
            be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
          #KTheoreticQuantization.num_defn
            %h6#definition_10 Definition
            %p
              Given a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+structure"} spin^c structure
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi τ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat \tau_X
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              whose underlying
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/determinant+line+bundle"} determinant line bundle
              coincides, up to equivalence, with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mi ω
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (L_\omega)^{\otimes^2}
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+quantization"} spin^c quantization
              of this prequantum data is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index"} index
              of the corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+Dirac+operator"} spin^c Dirac operator
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi D
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_{\mathbf{c}}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℋ
                      %mi K
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi index
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{H}_{K} \coloneqq index(D_{\mathbf{c}})
                    \\,.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_10 Remark
            %p
              This is equivalently the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+integration+in+generalized+cohomology"} push-forward
              in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+K-theory"} complex K-theory
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
              to the point.
            %p
              Moreover, on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycles"} cocycles
              this push-forward is expressed by the traditional construction of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} of quantum states
            %p
              let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be a manifold equipped with a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor+bundle"} spinor bundle
              and a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \colon \Gamma(S) \to \Gamma(S)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-homology"} K-homology
              cycle is:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi L
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^2(S)
                  of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/square+integrable+function"} square integrable
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
                  of the
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor+bundle"} spinor bundle
              %li
                %p
                  equipped with the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
                  by fiberwise multiplication of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebra"} C*-algebra
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_0(X)
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+functions"} of functions
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vanishing+at+infinity"} vanishing at infinity
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi ℬ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi L
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        C_0(X) \to \mathcal{B}(L^2(S))
                %p
                  (which is by
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+operators"} bounded operators
              %li
                %p
                  and equipped with the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                  as the
                  = succeed "-graded" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mn 2
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fredholm+operator"} Fredholm operator
                  defining the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-homology"} K-homology
                  class.
            %p Together with is equivalent an element
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi D
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi KK
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    [D, L^2(S)] \in KK(C_0(X), \mathbb{C})
                    \\,.
            %p
              Postcomposition of K-theory classes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ξ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi KK
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\xi] \in KK(\mathbb{C}, C_0(X))
              with this map is the
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+integration+in+generalized+cohomology"} push-forward
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index"} index
              map in K-theory.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_11 Remark
            %p
              This definition does not assume any choice of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarization"} polarization
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+structure"} complex structure
              etc. On the other hand, every choice of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/almost+complex+structure"} almost complex structure
              (hence in particular of
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+polarization"} Kähler polarization
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+structure"} spin^c structure
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "#BorthwickUribe96"} Borthwick-Uribe 96
          %p So then we can compare:
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              If a choice of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+polarization"} Kähler polarization
              exists on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+geometry"} prequantum geometry
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mi ω
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,L_\omega)
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#KTheoreticQuantization"}
              coincides with the Dolbeault-Dirac quantization of def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DolbeaultDiracSpacesOfStates"}
              and hence, by remark
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DolbeaultDiracAgreesWithEuler"}
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EulerQuantumStates"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+quantum+states"} spaces of quantum states
              agree:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi ω
                        %mo )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi Kaehler
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msub
                        %mi ℋ
                        %mi K
                      %mo ≃
                      %msub
                        %mi ℋ
                        %mi DolbDirac
                      %mo ≃
                      %msub
                        %mi ℋ
                        %mi Euler
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\left(\left(X,\omega\right) \; Kaehler\right)
                    \\;\Rightarrow\;
                    \\left(
                    \\mathcal{H}_K
                    \\simeq
                    \\mathcal{H}_{DolbDirac}
                    \\simeq
                    \\mathcal{H}_{Euler}
                    \\right)
                    \\,.
          %p
            This is to some extent discussed for instance in (
            %a{:href => "#Hochs08"}> Hochs 08, lemma 3.32
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Paradan09"} Paradan 09, prop. 2.2
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sqrt{\Omega^{n,0}}
              be a choice of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/square+root"} square root
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Theta+characteristic"}> Theta characteristic
              ) of the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+line+bundle"} canonical line bundle
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SpinStructuresOnKaehlerManifolds"}
              is a choice of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} spin structure
              in the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+manifold"} Kähler manifold
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor+bundle"} spinor bundle
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
              to
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mo •
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    S \simeq \Omega^{0,\bullet} \otimes \sqrt{\Omega^{n,0}}
            %p
              and so the corresponding
              = succeed "-twisted" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\omega
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi S
                            %mo ⊗
                            %msub
                              %mi L
                              %mi ω
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≃
                            %mrow
                              %mo (
                              %msup
                                %mi Ω
                                %mrow
                                  %mn 0
                                  %mo ,
                                  %mo •
                              %mo ⊗
                              %msqrt
                                %mrow
                                  %msup
                                    %mi Ω
                                    %mrow
                                      %mi n
                                      %mo ,
                                      %mn 0
                              %mo )
                            %mo ⊗
                            %msub
                              %mi L
                              %mi ω
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≃
                            %msup
                              %mi Ω
                              %mrow
                                %mn 0
                                %mo ,
                                %mo •
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mrow
                              %mo (
                              %msqrt
                                %mrow
                                  %msup
                                    %mi Ω
                                    %mrow
                                      %mi n
                                      %mo ,
                                      %mn 0
                              %mo ⊗
                              %msub
                                %mi L
                                %mi ω
                              %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    S \otimes L_\omega &amp; \simeq
                    \\left(\Omega^{0,\bullet} \otimes \sqrt{\Omega^{n,0}}\right)
                    \\otimes L_\omega
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \simeq
                    \\Omega^{0,\bullet} \otimes \left(\sqrt{\Omega^{n,0}}
                    \\otimes L_\omega\right)
                    \\end{aligned}
                    \\,,
            %p
              where we have re-bracheted just for emphasis of how the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metaplectic+correction"} metaplectic correction
              appears as part of the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor+bundle"} spinor bundle
            %p
              At the same time, by remark
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SpinCSpinorsAsTensorProductWhenDetDivisible"}
              we have that under this assumption that an actual spin structure exists, the
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Spin
                        %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spin^c
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor+bundle"} spinor bundle
              is isomorphic to
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mi c
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mi ω
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    S^c \simeq S \otimes L_\omega
                    \\,.
            %p
              So the two spinor bundles agree. It is now sufficient to observe that under this identification both the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dolbeault-Dirac+operator"} Dolbeault-Dirac operator
              as well as the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+Dirac+operator"} spin^c Dirac operator
              have the same
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symbol+of+a+differential+operator"} symbol
              to conclude that they have the same
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index"} index
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_12 Remark
            %p
              The assumption in the above that we do have a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} spin structure
              is only for comparison with the Euler-/ Dolbeault-quantization. It is not necessary for the K-theoretic geometric quantization by
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+structure"} spin^c structure
            %p
              In the general case the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/determinant+line+bundle"} determinant line bundle
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+structure"} spin^c structure
              may not admit a square root. (Its failure of having a square root will be compensated precisely by the failure of there being a genuine
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} spin structure
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index"} index
              of the corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+structure"} spin^c structure
              is like a quantization of a would-be square root of the determinant line bundle in this case.
          %h4#QuantumOperators Quantum operators / observables
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+action"} Hamiltonian action
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization+by+push-forward"} K-theoretic quantization by push-foward
            in
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+K-theory"} equivariant K-theory
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  K_G(\ast) \simeq K(\ast//G) \simeq R(G)
          %p
            This is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+ring"} representation ring
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            and hence yields not just a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+operators"} quantum operators
            = succeed "-actions." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            needs to be oriented not just in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+structure"}> spin^c structure
            ) but in
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+K-theory"} equivariant K-theory
            (equivariant
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+structure"} spin^c structure
          %p
            So the geometric quantization of observables is essentially what mathematically is known as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+induction"} Dirac induction
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#DependenceOnChoiceOfPolarization Functorial dependence on choices
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Knizhnik-Zamolodchikov+connection"} Knizhnik-Zamolodchikov connection
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hitchin+connection"} Hitchin connection
            %li
              %p
                L. Charles,
                %em Semi-classical properties of geometric quantization with metaplectic correction
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0602168"}> arXiv:math/0602168
                )
          %p Chapter 3 of
          %ul#Lauridsen10
            %li
              Lauridsen,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Aspects of quantum mathematics – Hitchin connections and the AJ conjecture
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://pure.au.dk/portal/files/41741925/imf_phd_2010_mrl.pdf"} pdf
          %h3#compatibility_of_quantization_with_symplectic_reduction Compatibility of quantization with symplectic reduction
          %p
            On the relation between geometric quantization and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+reduction"} symplectic reduction
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Guillemin-Sternberg+geometric+quantization+conjecture"} Guillemin-Sternberg geometric quantization conjecture
          %h3#characteristic_central_extensions Characteristic central extensions
          %p
            To a large extent geometric quantization is realized by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/central+extension"} central extension
            of various Lie groups arising in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
          %div
            %p
              %strong>
                higher and integrated
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kostant-Souriau+extensions"} Kostant-Souriau extensions
              \:
            %p
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-group+extension"} ∞-group extension
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-group+of+bisections"} ∞-group of bisections
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+Atiyah+groupoid"} higher Atiyah groupoid
              for
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔾
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{G}
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-connection"} principal ∞-connection
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
